%I A397105 #22 Sep 14 2026 15:54:52
%S A397105 0,1,6,36,189,972,4860,24057,118098,577368,2814669,13699368,66607272,
%T A397105 323647569,1572002478,7633618524,37063226781,179935293780,
%U A397105 873504062532,4240317252105,20583650580570,99917293795056,485015196963501,2354332616105616,11428228764167184
%N A397105 a(n) = 3^(n-1)*(Fibonacci(n+2) - 1).
%C A397105 In the paper's notation, a(n) = C_n^S, the number of product-centered local equilateral triangles contained in the no-e support S_n = {i,j,k}^n in order-n floretion triangular coordinates.
%C A397105 The admissible nonempty selection patterns are the binary words of length n assembled from blocks 0 and 11, with the possibility of one final unpaired 1. There are Fibonacci(n+2)-1 such patterns, and each contributes 3^(n-1) local triangles.
%C A397105 Locality is exhaustive on S_n, so this also counts all product-centered equilateral triangles contained in S_n.
%C A397105 For example, in order 3, T = {jii,jjj,jkk} = T(jii,{2,3}) is a product-centered local triangle in S_3; its product point and Euclidean center both equal P(jee).
%H A397105 Creighton Dement, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.15925">Equilateral Completion in Floretion Triangular Coordinates: Locality, Product Points, and Reflection Symmetry</a>, arXiv:2608.15925 [math.CO], 2026. See Appendix C.
%H A397105 Creighton Dement, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.20265">Bitwise triangular coordinates for central products of quaternion groups</a>, arXiv:2605.20265 [math.GM], 2026.
%H A397105 Creighton Dement, <a href="https://www.floretions.com">Online Floretion Calculator</a>.
%H A397105 <a href="/index/Rec#order_03">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (6,0,-27).
%F A397105 a(n) = (A000045(n+2) - 1)*3^(n-1).
%F A397105 a(n) = A000071(n+2) * A140429(n).
%F A397105 a(n) = 6*a(n-1) - 27*a(n-3), n >= 3, with a(0)=0, a(1)=1, a(2)=6.
%F A397105 G.f.: x/((1 - 3*x)*(1 - 3*x - 9*x^2)).
%e A397105 a(3) = 36: there are Fibonacci(5)-1 = 4 admissible nonempty selection patterns, and each contributes 3^(3-1) = 9 local triangles.
%p A397105 a:= n-> (<<0|1|0>, <0|0|1>, <-27|0|6>>^n.<<0, 1, 6>>)[1,1]:
%p A397105 seq(a(n), n=0..24);  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Sep 08 2026
%o A397105 (PARI) a(n) = 3^(n-1)*(fibonacci(n+2) - 1); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Sep 08 2026
%Y A397105 Cf. A000045, A000071, A016137, A016150, A105693, A140429.
%K A397105 nonn,easy,new
%O A397105 0,3
